an investigation into a deadly  fraternity boxing match points to a  failure by metro police and a lack of  cooperation from the other boxer this is  a story our i-team first broke thank you  for joining us i'm brian loftis i'm  vanessa murphy 8 news now is the first  to obtain a report with some shocking  findings which the nevada state athletic  commission will address tomorrow morning  from the planning to the event to the  aftermath this 158-page report from the  nevada attorney general's office points  to numerous failures surrounding this  deadly fraternity boxing match in  november  twenty-year-old nathan valencia seen  here in the red headgear took fatal  blows to the head he collapsed moments  after the fight i told him you're going  to be ok like just keep pushing through  it and i thought he was going to be ok  danielle corona is one of the more than  dozen interviewed by investigators their  three key findings not all fighters were  students which led to confusion over  what agency could regulate the fight  metro police failed to investigate  whether foul play was involved the  report states the department only looked  into whether the venue had a business  license while there was a missed  opportunity to learn whether valencia's  opponent was on drugs or tampered with  his hand wraps or gloves by packing them  as suggested by several witnesses and  metro's public statements eliminated any  possibility for future prosecution it  was just fighting like there was no like  boxing involved of it david cruz shot  this video mentioned in the report where  an unlicensed referee is seen drinking  what appears to be alcohol one witness  stated they were offered cocaine at the  event paramedics said when they arrived  two groups they encountered appear to be  intoxicated i think there is somebody to  blame here and i think it's  the fraternity for  organizing a poor event and  not ensuring the safety of  their fighters usa boxing's executive  director told investigators the kappa  sigma fraternity was repeatedly warned  about the danger of holding events like  this one investigators determined  numerous protocols weren't followed no  pre-fight weigh-in or medical physical  no certified ref or judges no doctor at  ringside and no ambulance on standby no  inspection of hand wraps gloves or  headgear investigators say they examined  gear used at the event except for  valencia's opponents witnesses said  emanuel ailman's hands were wrapped long  before the fight his attorney sean  claggett confirmed to the i-team he told  aleman not to speak with investigators  while investigators say even claggett  didn't return their calls according to  the report a unlv detective said he  spoke with illman's stepfather who  denied any tampering with the gloves or  hand wraps he called the punches thrown  girl swings and stated sometimes people  die that's why nathan valencia's loved  ones have been in mourning  it's tough it's really tough this  because he just he was a great guy  emmanuel ailman's attorneys say he was  not under the influence of an illicit  substance during the fight and he did  not pack his gloves they say they told  him not to speak with investigators  because of the investigator's reliance  on misrepresentation and metro police  just sent me a statement disputing the  findings of the attorney general's  office you can find the full statements  at eight newsnow dot com
 

